  ## Read from the renders of `183:10675` and `186:10737`, 2026-10-13

  Metadata carries no colour, no corner, no border and no weight, and
  `MANIFEST.md` records that a stroke on a frame is not a child layer. These
  were read from the two renders instead, which is the same way the sign-in
  screen's error border was read.

  **A choice row is a card, not a field.** 600 x 85, `bg/surface` white, a
  hairline `border/default`, and a corner of the same family as the sign-in
  entry's. Inside it, the Title at (21, 17) in `text/primary` and the
  Description at (21, 46) in `text/muted`.

  The Description is **whatever that row is for**: on *Your name* it is the
  name as typed — `disan`, which is sample content — and on *New PIN* it is
  the invitation *Enter your PIN*. So the row is the same component doing the
  work the sign-in entry does, with its label above rather than inside.

  **The two actions are not the same shape.** The first is filled: a navy
  ground, `text/primary` inverted to the warm off-white, the same corner. The
  second is outlined: the screen's own ground with a hairline and ink
  lettering. First is *Continue* and *Save PIN*; second is *Back* and *Use
  password only*.

  **The eyebrow's casing is the content's, not the component's.** *MAKE IT
  YOURS* is upper on one sheet and *Optional · PIN* is sentence case on the
  other, at the same size and colour. Nothing should upper-case it in code.

  **The title wraps.** *What should we call you?* is two lines at 88 tall;
  *Add a PIN?* is one at 44. So the column's height is the content's and the
  blocks below it move — which is what the two frames' differing `Main`
  heights, 459 and 440, already say.

  **The stage line reads `alo OS`** at the top right, and the footer reads
  *Local setup. No online account required.* at the bottom right, muted. Both
  are the same on both sheets.

  ## Still owed

  The exact hexes and corner radii are **not** recorded here, because reading
  a colour off a render is a guess at an anti-aliased pixel. They should come
  from `variables.toml`'s roles — `bg/surface`, `border/default`,
  `text/primary`, `text/muted` — which `crate::sign_in_surface::TheColours`
  already answers per scheme and contrast.
